  "coding-08-kill-the-mutants":{category:"coding",title:"Test suite that kills every mutant",objective:[["obj-1","`node tests.js` runs against the embedded reference `normalize` and every printed line is PASS"],["obj-2","The suite includes a test that distinguishes the all-equal case (e.g. asserts normalize([5,5,5]) deep-equals [0,0,0]), which fails on M1 (M1 yields [NaN,NaN,NaN], i.e. [null,null,null] under JSON round-trip)"],["obj-3","The suite includes an empty-input test asserting normalize([]) deep-equals [], which fails on M3 (M3 yields [0])"],["obj-4","The suite includes a negative-values test (normalize([-2,0,2]) deep-equals [0,0.5,1]) that fails on M2 (M2 yields [0,1,2]) AND a test that fails on M4 (M4 yields the sign-flipped [-1,-0.75,-0.5,-0.25,0] on [1,2,3,4,5], distinct from the reference [0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1]) — judge splices each of the 4 mutants in and confirms >=1 failing test per mutant"],["obj-5","tests.js is a single dependency-free file (its own assert helper, no test-runner import) at most 120 lines and does not import or re-copy the mutants"]],subjective:[["sub-quality","Mutation coverage",0.4],["sub-craft","Test design and readability",0.3],["sub-reasoning","Reasoning quality",0.3]],weights:{objective:0.5,subjective:0.5}},
