  "security-08-input-validator-authoring":{category:"cybersecurity",title:"Guard the handle field for Spinnaker Social",objective:[["obj-1","The judge imports validateHandle from the runner's validate.js and runs its OWN labeled fixture: every accept case returns true and every reject case returns false, including 'abc\\\\nrm' -> false, 'BAD ok' -> false, 'a__b' -> false, 'ab_' -> false, ' ab' -> false, 'Abc' -> false, 'ab' -> false, '9abc' -> false, and 'a1_b' -> true"],["obj-2","The judge calls validateHandle('abc\\\\nrm') directly on the imported module and it returns false (isolates the m-flag trap independent of the harness)"],["obj-3","The validation regex in validate.js is fully anchored with ^ and $ and does NOT use the m (multiline) flag (judge inspects the regex literal); an unanchored regex or one with the m flag fails"],["obj-4","The judge calls validateHandle('a__b') and validateHandle('ab_') directly and both return false (consecutive/trailing underscore rejected) — a plain [a-z0-9_] character class alone does not satisfy this"],["obj-5","NOTES.md explains at least one of the two JS-specific traps (why the m flag would wrongly accept an embedded newline, OR why an unanchored regex would accept junk with a valid substring). Judge-decided by reading."]],subjective:[["sub-quality","Validation completeness and correctness",0.4],["sub-craft","Defensive-parsing rationale",0.3],["sub-reasoning","Reasoning quality",0.3]],weights:{objective:0.7,subjective:0.3}},
